  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c7390940",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### what each column means\n",
    "\n",
    "**Age**: Numeric - participant's age in years (18-64, all adults).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Gender**: Categorical - Male / Female / Other.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Occupation**: Categorical - participant's job (13 categories: Student, Doctor, Engineer, Artist, etc).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sleep Hours**: Numeric - average hours of sleep per night.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Physical Activity (hrs/week)**: Numeric - hours of physical activity per week.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Caffeine Intake (mg/day)**: Numeric - daily caffeine intake in mg (for reference, one cup of coffee is roughly 95mg).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Alcohol Consumption (drinks/week)**: Numeric - number of alcoholic drinks per week.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Smoking**: Categorical (Binary) - whether the participant smokes (Yes/No).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Family History of Anxiety**: Categorical (Binary) - whether anxiety runs in the family (Yes/No).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Stress Level (1-10)**: Ordinal - self-rated stress, 1 = completely relaxed, 10 = crisis.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Heart Rate (bpm)**: Numeric - heart rate in beats per minute.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Breathing Rate (breaths/min)**: Numeric - breaths per minute.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sweating Level (1-5)**: Ordinal - self-rated sweating, 1 = dry, 5 = profuse/drenched.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Dizziness**: Categorical (Binary) - whether the participant experiences dizziness (Yes/No).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Medication**: Categorical (Binary) - whether the participant is on medication (Yes/No).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Therapy Sessions (per month)**: Numeric - how many therapy sessions attended per month (0 if none).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Recent Major Life Event**: Categorical (Binary) - whether something big happened recently (Yes/No) - breakup, job loss, move, etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Diet Quality (1-10)**: Ordinal - self-rated diet, 1 = mostly junk food, 10 = consistently nutrient-dense.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Therapy History**: Categorical - what kind of therapy they've had before (CBT, Group Therapy, Psychodynamic Therapy, No previous history). Mostly missing (~90%), probably just wasn't asked/applicable for most people.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Anxiety Level (1-10)**: Ordinal - self-rated anxiety, 1 = calm, 10 = panic level.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Target / is_Anxious**: Categorical (Binary) - the target variable, whether the participant is classified as anxious (1) or not (0). these two columns are identical.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d03a77a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Stress Level (1–10)\n",
    "A simple self‑rating tool where a person estimates how stressed they feel right now. It’s subjective, but using clear anchors helps keep ratings consistent.\n",
    "\n",
    "1 – Completely relaxed  \n",
    "No noticeable stress. Calm, clear thinking, body relaxed.\n",
    "\n",
    "2 – Very mild tension  \n",
    "Slight pressure but easily manageable.\n",
    "\n",
    "3 – Low stress  \n",
    "Aware of some demands, but still comfortable and functioning well.\n",
    "\n",
    "4 – Noticeable stress  \n",
    "You feel somewhat pressured or distracted.\n",
    "\n",
    "5 – Moderate stress  \n",
    "Stress is clearly present; concentration takes more effort.\n",
    "\n",
    "6 – Elevated stress  \n",
    "Feeling tense or irritable; performance may start to drop.\n",
    "\n",
    "7 – High stress  \n",
    "Difficult to focus, physical symptoms may appear (tight shoulders, faster heart rate).\n",
    "\n",
    "8 – Very high stress  \n",
    "Overwhelmed; thinking becomes less clear and emotions stronger.\n",
    "\n",
    "9 – Extreme stress  \n",
    "Close to panic or shutdown; functioning is very difficult.\n",
    "\n",
    "10 – Maximum stress / crisis  \n",
    "Panic, breakdown, or inability to cope.\n",
    "\n",
    "How people usually measure it:\n",
    "- Self‑check: “Where am I right now on the 1–10 scale?”\n",
    "- Body cues: heart rate, muscle tension, breathing, headaches.\n",
    "- Mental cues: racing thoughts, inability to focus, irritability.\n",
    "- Behavior cues: avoidance, snapping at people, mistakes.\n",
    "\n",
    "Many therapists and coaches ask clients to rate stress before and after an activity (meeting, workout, breathing exercise) to see what helps.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Sweating Level (1–5)\n",
    "\n",
    "1 – None / Dry  \n",
    "Skin is dry. No noticeable sweating.\n",
    "\n",
    "2 – Slight  \n",
    "Very light moisture on skin (forehead, palms, or underarms), but not visible to others.\n",
    "\n",
    "3 – Moderate  \n",
    "Clearly noticeable sweat. Skin feels damp; small sweat spots may appear on clothing.\n",
    "\n",
    "4 – Heavy  \n",
    "Visible sweating and larger sweat patches on clothing. Sweat may drip or require wiping.\n",
    "\n",
    "5 – Extreme / Profuse  \n",
    "Constant dripping or soaking clothing. Sweat runs down face or body.\n",
    "\n",
    "This type of scale is often used in exercise studies, heat stress monitoring, or medical observations to quickly rate sweating intensity.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Diet Quality (1–10)\n",
    "\n",
    "1 – Very poor  \n",
    "Mostly ultra‑processed foods, sugary drinks, fast food, little to no fruits or vegetables.\n",
    "\n",
    "2 – Poor  \n",
    "Frequent junk food and refined carbs; very low nutrient variety.\n",
    "\n",
    "3 – Below average  \n",
    "Some real food, but still heavy in processed items and added sugars.\n",
    "\n",
    "4 – Somewhat unhealthy  \n",
    "Occasional fruits/vegetables but inconsistent balance; many refined foods.\n",
    "\n",
    "5 – Average  \n",
    "Mixed diet: some healthy meals, some unhealthy ones.\n",
    "\n",
    "6 – Fairly good  \n",
    "Regular fruits/vegetables and home‑cooked meals, but still room for improvement.\n",
    "\n",
    "7 – Good  \n",
    "Balanced meals most of the time; decent protein, whole grains, fruits, and vegetables.\n",
    "\n",
    "8 – Very good  \n",
    "Mostly whole foods, good nutrient balance, limited processed foods.\n",
    "\n",
    "9 – Excellent  \n",
    "Highly balanced diet with plenty of whole foods, healthy fats, lean proteins, fiber.\n",
    "\n",
    "10 – Optimal  \n",
    "Consistently nutrient‑dense, varied diet aligned with strong nutrition guidelines (lots of whole foods, minimal processed items).\n",
    "\n",
    "People often estimate this based on factors like:\n",
    "- fruit and vegetable intake  \n",
    "- whole vs processed foods  \n",
    "- sugar and sugary drink consumption  \n",
    "- protein quality  \n",
    "- variety of nutrients  \n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Difference Between Stress Levels and Anxiety Levels\n",
    "\n",
    "Stress and anxiety are related but not the same.\n",
    "\n",
    "Stress\n",
    "- Usually a response to a specific external pressure (deadline, exam, argument, workload).\n",
    "- Often temporary and improves when the situation passes.\n",
    "- More tied to current demands.\n",
    "- Symptoms: tension, irritability, trouble concentrating, headaches, fatigue.\n",
    "- Example: feeling stressed because you have a project due tomorrow.\n",
    "\n",
    "Anxiety\n",
    "- More about worry, fear, or anticipation of possible threats, sometimes without a clear trigger.\n",
    "- Can persist even when no immediate stressor exists.\n",
    "- Often involves excessive worrying about the future.\n",
    "- Symptoms: constant worry, restlessness, racing thoughts, rapid heartbeat, sleep problems.\n",
    "- Example: worrying for days that you might fail something even when nothing immediate is happening.\n",
    "\n",
    "Simple way to think about it\n",
    "- Stress: pressure from a situation.  \n",
    "- Anxiety: the internal feeling of fear or worry, sometimes continuing after the stressor is gone.\n",
    "\n",
    "They often overlap: prolonged stress can lead to anxiety, and anxiety can make normal stress feel much stronger.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Anxiety Level (1–10)\n",
    "\n",
    "1 – No anxiety  \n",
    "Calm, relaxed, no worry.\n",
    "\n",
    "2 – Very mild  \n",
    "Slight uneasiness but barely noticeable.\n",
    "\n",
    "3 – Mild  \n",
    "Occasional worry or nervousness, but it doesn’t affect functioning.\n",
    "\n",
    "4 – Noticeable  \n",
    "Anxiety is present and distracting at times.\n",
    "\n",
    "5 – Moderate  \n",
    "Frequent worry; concentration takes effort.\n",
    "\n",
    "6 – Elevated  \n",
    "Persistent anxious thoughts, some physical symptoms (restlessness, muscle tension).\n",
    "\n",
    "7 – High  \n",
    "Difficult to focus; clear physical symptoms like faster heartbeat or shallow breathing.\n",
    "\n",
    "8 – Very high  \n",
    "Strong worry, feeling overwhelmed, hard to think clearly.\n",
    "\n",
    "9 – Severe  \n",
    "Near panic; racing thoughts, strong physical discomfort.\n",
    "\n",
    "10 – Extreme / panic level  \n",
    "Panic attack or feeling completely unable to cope.\n",
    "\n",
    "People often judge their number using:\n",
    "- Thoughts: racing worries, fear, rumination  \n",
    "- Body signals: heart rate, sweating, tension, nausea  \n",
    "- Functioning: how much anxiety interferes with tasks or decisions.\n"
   ]
  },
